<div class="fullrow"><p>
    Games in this tier have Epiphany Game elements, but it is not the core experience.
</p></div>
<div class="fullrow"><div class="tierlist">
{%- for game in collections.glowBorderline %}
{% include "glowsummary.njk" %}
{%- endfor %}
</div></div>

<h2>Games That Don't Quite Fit</h2>
<div class="fullrow"><div class="tierlist">
{%- for game in collections.glowNotGLOW %}
{% include "glowsummary.njk" %}
{%- endfor %}
</div></div>
<p>
    And others I don't have full reviews for:
</p>
<p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            {{ externalLink("https://store.steampowered.com/app/736260/Baba_Is_You/", "Baba is You") -}}:
            Is a fantastic puzzle game
            {%- call sidenote("sn-puzzle") %}
            There is overlap between puzzle games and Epiphany Games.
            A good puzzle demands that the player fully explore the problem,
            just like you would explore the solar system of Outer Wilds.
            The difference, I suppose, is whether you're exploring a world someone designed, or raw math.
            That said, many Epiphany Games are also puzzle games.
            Check out
            {{ externalLink("https://thinkygames.com/lists/thinky-essentials/", "Thinky Essentials") }}
            for a good list of puzzle games with a bias toward epiphanies.
            {% endcall -%}.
            I was close to adding it to the list, but I do not quite consider it an Epiphany Game.
            The epiphanies in the later puzzles are only a small part of the experience.
        </li>
        <li>
            {{ externalLink("https://store.steampowered.com/app/1677770/The_Case_of_the_Golden_Idol/", "The Case of the Golden Idol") -}}:
            A detective game like Obra Dinn, but the player has less agency, and the twists are mostly limited to discrete short levels.
            There is one big reveal, but - at the risk of offending those who didn't see it - it's really obvious.
        <li>
            {{ externalLink("https://playcebo.itch.io/12-word-searches", "12 word searches") -}}:
            This actually could go in the Puzzle Box tier,
            but being a short pen-and-paper game, it isn't the same scope as the games in the list.
            I really liked it, though!
        </li>
        <li>
            {{ externalLink("https://letibus.bigcartel.com/product/lok", "LOK") -}}:
            Another pen-and-paper game, this one is foremost a book of puzzles rather than an Epiphany Game.
            While you must learn the rules yourself, the rules don't change your understanding of earlier puzzles.
        </li>        
    </ul>
</p>

